Blockchain-assisted federated learning has emerged as a promising paradigm
	for privacy-preserving collaborative training across IoT devices. However,
	most existing frameworks treat malicious behavior detection, trust evaluation,
	and economic incentives as independent modules, making it difficult to
	establish clear causal links between a participant's actions and the
	resulting system-wide consequences.
	To address this limitation, this paper presents BRITE-FL, a federated
	learning framework that unifies behavioral evidence, reputation dynamics,
	and token-based settlement into a single closed-loop workflow governed
	by on-chain smart contracts.
	The framework separates heterogeneous IoT devices into training and
	validation roles, where validators independently score each submitted
	update and reach detection consensus through a majority-based protocol.
	The detection outcome is fed into a reputation mechanism with
	exponentially escalating penalties, which in turn determines each
	node's deposit requirement, reward share, and continued eligibility,
	forming a self-reinforcing closed loop between behavioral evidence
	and economic consequences.
	Experiments on MNIST and Fashion-MNIST under data poisoning and label
	flipping attacks with up to 30\% malicious participants show that
	BRITE-FL limits model accuracy degradation to within 5\% and achieves
	a detection rate exceeding 94\%, outperforming four baseline schemes.
	Blockchain integration introduces less than 9\% overhead per training
	round, with median transaction confirmation delay below 0.7 seconds
	at scales up to 1000 nodes.
	These results demonstrate that tightly coupling behavioral evidence
	with on-chain reputation and economic enforcement provides an effective
	and scalable approach to securing federated learning in heterogeneous
	IoT environments.
